17592186527454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a857d99b-4347-5fe1-ae63-d9fb653c5ffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186527773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186527474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527890 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4f068c8-83d6-567f-8453-a2683cd9c143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-218679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/386b2307d81c8f0aa895a192ac2734a7b24f122edb26229f3f9c10f0941c96e7.png)\n\nThe average rate of change of the curve shown above, over the interval $[0,4]$, is $\\frac{Δy}{Δx}=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5381d86b-69c1-53d6-ab4d-bc350eeb1469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527942 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we can see that $f(0)=-2$ and $f(4)=1.$\n\nApplying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-2)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1071 "The ice level in the North Pole is decreasing due to global warming. The thickness of ice, measured in feet, in the North Pole, is represented by the expression $135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2},$ where $x$ is the time, in years, since measurements began. What is the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c25ff0b-f786-5f82-b2d5-6e5163b97efc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527960 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2}$ be the thickness of the ice, in feet, after $x$ years. To find the average rate of change of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,10]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(0)}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[135+0.1(10)-0.02(10)^{2}]-[135+0.1(0)-0.02(0)^{2}]}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{134-135}{10} \\\\  & =-0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years is $0.1$ feet per year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-6\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-7\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5d8a95f-b00e-51c0-82a6-3182a1f44e55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527984 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=f(4)=-5,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-4,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1071 "The height $h(t),$ measured in feet, of a plant after $t$ years is given by the expression $h(t)=3{t}^{3/2}-2t.$ What is the average rate of growth over the first $4$ years?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6dc0dd6-8281-5909-b3f4-b0dcdc2e7ecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527985 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h(t)=3{t}^{3/2}-2t,$ the height measured in feet, of a plant after $t$ years. To find the average rate of change of $h(t)$ from $t=0$ to $t=4,$ we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,4]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δh}{Δt} & =\\frac{h(b)-h(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(4)-h(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3({4}^{3/2})-2(4)]-[3({0}^{3/2})-2(0)]}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-8}{4} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth is $4$ feet per year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8be6849-5f2a-595e-8c72-25b5d6a335d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527986 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-1)=f(5)=3,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-1,5].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below lists values of $f(x)$ at distinct $x$-values. Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[2p,2]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-9\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,2p\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-12\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc7ad0f1-6b84-5b13-8e8e-b050839dc127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527987 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(2p)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-(-4)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2-2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[2p,2].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2-2p}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{2-2p} & =3 \\\\ 12 & =3\\cdot (2-2p) \\\\ 12 & =6-6p \\\\ 12-6 & =-6p \\\\ 6 & =-6p \\\\ \\frac{6}{-6} & =\\frac{-6p}{-6} \\\\ -1 & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined as $y=3x-5.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[3,7].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dba8d021-2c20-52de-b1fb-abd8fe5f7a55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528001 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=3x-5.$ Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(3)}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3(7)-5]-[3(3)-5]]}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-4}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,1]$ given the following table of values for $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc25546e-e94f-545f-9768-cf226be3dc76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528002 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the averge rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)-f(-3)}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-2}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
